From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leon Zandman <leon@wirwar.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:04:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] 9993: Parent dialogs to the tool's last active window when no
 window is active

When a dialog is shown without a parent and Java reports no active window,
getParentWindow() fell back to the tool's root frame. On macOS this happens
after Delete Block in a detached Memory Map window: the progress dialog is
shown right after the confirmation dialog closes, before the OS has made the
Memory Map window active again. The progress dialog got the CodeBrowser frame
as its owner, and that frame came to the front when the dialog closed.

getParentWindow() now tries the tool's last active window before the root
frame. The existing walk up the component hierarchy moved into
getNonTransientWindowFor(), so a closed, modal or transient last window
resolves to its owner, and windows that cannot be parents are still skipped.
Use case 'F' in the method's comment describes the new rule.

DialogParentFallbackTest covers the fallback with a fake focus manager.

Fixes #9963
---
 .../java/docking/DockingWindowManager.java    |  19 +-
 .../docking/DialogParentFallbackTest.java     | 183 ++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 201 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 Ghidra/Framework/Docking/src/test.slow/java/docking/DialogParentFallbackTest.java

diff --git a/Ghidra/Framework/Docking/src/main/java/docking/DockingWindowManager.java b/Ghidra/Framework/Docking/src/main/java/docking/DockingWindowManager.java
index ba8379c2f6..67ac9bab4c 100644
--- a/Ghidra/Framework/Docking/src/main/java/docking/DockingWindowManager.java
+++ b/Ghidra/Framework/Docking/src/main/java/docking/DockingWindowManager.java
@@ -2171,6 +2171,12 @@ public class DockingWindowManager implements PropertyChangeListener, Placeholder
 		 		E -A long-running API shows a non-modal progress dialog.  This API then shows a
 		 		   results dialog which is also non-modal.  We do not want to parent the new dialog
 		 		   to the original dialog, since it is a progress dialog that will go away.
+				F -The user acts in a detached window, a dialog closes, and a new dialog is shown
+				   before the OS has made the detached window active again, so Java reports no
+				   active window.  The new dialog should use the window the user last worked in,
+				   not the main tool frame, or that frame comes to the front when the dialog
+				   closes.  This applies to case 4 as well: with no active window, a background
+				   thread's dialog also uses the last active window.
 		
 		
 		 	For now, the easiest mental model to use is to always prefer the active non-transient
@@ -2183,9 +2189,20 @@ public class DockingWindowManager implements PropertyChangeListener, Placeholder
 		Window defaultWindow = dwm != null ? dwm.getRootFrame() : null;
 		if (parent == null) {
 			Window w = getActiveNonTransientWindow();
+			if (w == null && dwm != null) {
+				// Java reports no active window, for example while the OS is still activating a
+				// window after another one closed.  Use the window the user last worked in.  (See
+				// Use Case 'F' above.)
+				w = getNonTransientWindowFor(dwm.lastActiveWindow);
+			}
 			return w == null ? defaultWindow : w;
 		}
 
+		Window w = getNonTransientWindowFor(parent);
+		return w == null ? defaultWindow : w;
+	}
+
+	private static Window getNonTransientWindowFor(Component parent) {
 		Component c = parent;
 		while (c != null) {
 			// Note: using a Frame here means that we will not find and use a dialog that is a
@@ -2197,7 +2214,7 @@ public class DockingWindowManager implements PropertyChangeListener, Placeholder
 			}
 			c = c.getParent();
 		}
-		return defaultWindow;
+		return null;
 	}
 
 	private static boolean isNonTransientWindow(Component c) {
